In this society, moral responsibility is tethered to personal agency, chosen obligations, and direct rights. The family’s primary duty is to its own members, and its purchasing decisions are exercises of autonomous self-determination. Because the family did not create the exploitative supply chain and does not directly control the company’s operations, holding it morally accountable for overseas labor practices would violate the principle that obligations must follow from one’s own choices and actions. While the family’s purchase is a conscious choice, the dominant view is that individuals cannot be expected to police global systems through their private budgets, especially when doing so would impose a significant burden with no realistic effect. Thus, the most compelling consideration is that consumers are not responsible for supply chains they did not create, though the ineffectiveness of individual consumer action also carries real weight. Conversely, framing the purchase as a moral failure implies an unchosen duty to rectify distant structural wrongs, which sits uneasily with an autonomy-centered ethic.

When evaluating the reasoning behind the choice, what matters most is the family’s right to prioritize its own needs and budget. That right is the cornerstone of individual autonomy. It is also important that systemic problems like labor exploitation are properly addressed through systemic means—regulation, corporate accountability, and collective political action—rather than by saddling individual families with guilt for conditions they did not engineer. The idea that morality extends to every indirect economic consequence carries only minimal weight in this framework, because making individuals morally liable for endless attenuated outcomes would effectively deny them the freedom to act on their own rights and choices.

